---
import Essay from '../layouts/Essay.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import '../styles/chart.css';
---

<Essay
  docNo="03"
  title="How a product creates value"
  description="What engineering still decides once the ship date is fixed: cost committed before it is spent, the price of a late change, and what scale does to small numbers."
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p class="opener">If the ship date is fixed, what does engineering still decide?</p>
      <p>
        At a brand, the mass-production date is usually set when the project starts. Engineering
        does not decide when a product ships. It decides what the product carries when it does: its
        cost, its risk, and what the next one can learn.
      </p>
      <p>Three mechanisms shape that. None of them appears on a test report.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.1</span>
      <h2>Cost is committed before it is spent</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Material, process, tolerance, part count: each is chosen in design, and each sets a cost
        the factory pays on every unit. Most of a product's cost is decided while almost none of it
        has been spent.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="3.1"
        caption="Schematic, cumulative cost. Cost committed runs far ahead of cost incurred. The gap between the curves is where a decision is cheapest to change."
      >
