      <p>
        The figure usually quoted is that design commits about 70% of a product's cost. The exact
        number is disputed. The shape is not: commitment runs far ahead of spending.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        J. A. Barton, D. M. Love and G. D. Taylor (2001), "Design determines 70% of cost? A review of
        implications for design evaluation", J. Eng. Design 12(1), 47–58.
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/09544820010031553" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >Reference ↗</a
        >
      </p>
      <p><strong>The cheapest moment to create value is before anyone can see it.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.2</span>
      <h2>Every gate raises the price of a change</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        NPI moves in builds. EVT proves the design, DVT proves it against the specification, PVT
        proves the line can make it. Each build commits more: tooling is cut, parts are ordered,
        lines are set up.
      </p>
      <p>
        A change that is a drawing edit before EVT can mean new tooling after DVT, and a recall after
        ship. Quality engineers call it the rule of ten: a fix costs roughly ten times more at each
        stage it survives. It is a rule of thumb, not a law. The direction is never wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        The date rarely moves to make room for a late problem, and when it does, that has a price
        too. The problem is either fixed at a higher cost, or it ships as risk.
      </p>
      <p><strong>The later the change, the more expensive the decision.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.3</span>
      <h2>Scale turns small numbers into large ones</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        99% and 99.9% read as the same in a build report. At a million units, they are 10,000
        failures against 1,000.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yields multiply. Ten process steps at 99% each deliver about 90% end to end. Twenty deliver
        82%. Every step that looks fine on its own takes a share of the margin.
      </p>
      <p>
        Confidence has to survive scale. So does value. A defect rate is a unit cost, multiplied by
        volume.
      </p>
      <p><strong>At scale, every tenth of a percent is a line item.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <span class="sub__no">3.4</span>
      <h2>The question I bring</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        I am early in my career, and the ship date is not mine to set. What is mine is one question I
        bring to every result and every review: if this is wrong, where will we find out, and what
        will it cost by then?
      </p>
      <p>
        Each seat I have worked in answers part of it: the lab that produces evidence, the system
        company that builds, the brand that decides. Learning to answer all of it is the engineer I
        am working to become.
      </p>
      <p>Value is not decided at ship. It is priced at every gate before it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>
